[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The default gateway for 10.5.0.0/24 is the SVI on sshswitch (interface br_v5, IP 10.5.0.254). We tested from both affected clients and the gateway path shows no instability. From PC1 and PC2, 60 ICMP probes each to 10.5.0.254 returned 0% loss with sub-millisecond RTTs. On sshswitch, interface and bridge counters on br_v5 and eth0.5 show 0 errors and 0 drops, and “ip neigh” entries for 10.5.0.1/2/5 are stable. The bridge FDB anchors the gateway MAC (ae:af:cb:76:d1:8d) permanently on eth0.5 without movement, excluding MAC flaps. Kernel logs (dmesg) on sshswitch and the access switch (switch4) show only initial port state additions at startup and no link up/down events thereafter. The L2 link between switch4 (eth0) and sshswitch (eth0.5 into br_v5) is up, error-free, and not congested.

Affected component: None observed; the gateway SVI (br_v5 on sshswitch) and L2 path are healthy. Mechanism: If the gateway were flapping or overloaded, we would see ICMP loss, interface error counters, ARP churn, or link state changes; none are present. Localization: sshswitch br_v5 and switch4 eth0/eth0.5. Root cause: No device-side fault—reported intermittent loss is not attributable to the gateway interface. Corrective fix: No change required on the gateway; advise investigating client NIC/cabling, DNS, or upstream connectivity beyond the gateway and enabling temporary continuous monitoring (e.g., smokeping to 10.5.0.254 and switch/syslog traps) to correlate any future reports. 
=== END ===